### request 1
POST /v1/messages HTTP/1.1
x-api-key: <masked>
anthropic-version: 2023-06-01
content-type: application/json
accept: text/event-stream
anthropic-beta: server-side-fallback-2026-07-01,mid-conversation-output-config-2026-07-01,thinking-binding-controls-2026-08-01

{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","max_tokens":64000,"stream":true,"thinking":{"type":"adaptive","display":"summarized","block_binding":{"prefix_mismatch_behavior":"drop_block"}},"output_config":{"effort":"medium"},"cache_control":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"},"fallbacks":"default","tools":[{"description":"Ask the user for a secret (a password, an API key, a token) and have Wisp type it straight into a running process. A box opens where the user types or pastes it; you never see it and it never enters this conversation or the session log, so use this instead of asking in the chat. Give the number of the process that will read it. For a program that asks at a prompt (sudo, ssh, a login), run it with bash and tty, and call this once it waits at its prompt with echo off: the secret is typed and Enter pressed. For a command that reads the secret from standard input, such as wisp account add NAME --key-stdin or a password manager's command line, start it with bash and run_in_background, without tty, then call this with end_input true. For a command that wants the secret in an environment variable or inside other text, start a short script the same way that reads one line and passes it on (IFS= read -r S; TOKEN=\"$S\" exec the-command), and call this without end_input, since the script reads a whole line; never use one that puts the secret on a command line or prints it. If the result says the terminal would show what is typed, start the command again without tty so that it reads standard input; don't ask the user to take the terminal over for a secret. Make this the last call of your reply: calls after it in the same reply are not run. The result says only whether the secret was typed, never what it was.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"end_input":{"description":"Close the process's input after the secret, with no line break, for a command that reads standard input to its end. Defaults to false: the secret is followed by Enter.","type":"boolean"},"name":{"description":"What you are asking for, in a few words, shown as the box's title, such as \"sudo password\" or \"OpenAI API key\". At most 40 characters.","type":"string"},"process":{"description":"The number of the running process that reads the secret.","minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"reason":{"description":"One sentence shown to the user: why it is needed and what will be done with it. At most 200 characters.","type":"string"},"show_end":{"description":"How many characters at the end of a pasted secret the box shows. Defaults to 0.","maximum":16,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"show_start":{"description":"How many characters at the start of a pasted secret the box shows, so the user can check it is the right one. Leave this and show_end out for a password or passphrase: the box then shows only stars. For a key or token with a public prefix, give the prefix's length here (8 for sk-proj-) and 4 in show_end. For one with no public prefix, give 0 here and 4 in show_end. Wisp shows less when the secret is short, and only stars for one typed by hand. Defaults to 0.","maximum":16,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"}},"required":["name","process","reason"],"type":"object"},"name":"ask_secret"},{"description":"Ask the user one to four multiple-choice questions. Asking doesn't wait for the answers: the call returns at once, the questions stay on the user's screen, and the answers arrive later in a message of their own, whenever the user replies. Use it only for a decision that is genuinely the user's to make: one you can't settle from the request, the code or a sensible default, such as choosing between approaches or settling an ambiguous requirement. Don't use it for facts you can find out yourself, to ask permission or whether to proceed, or for choices with a conventional default: take the obvious one, say so and go on. Ask everything you need in one call, as the last call of your reply: calls after it in the same reply are not run. Only one set of questions is open at a time; while it is, go on with any work the answers don't decide, and end your turn when nothing is left. The user may see only your questions, not the text before the call, so each must stand alone: give the facts needed to answer it and never refer to \"above\". Give each question two to four distinct options; put the one you recommend first and end its label with \"(Recommended)\". The user can always type their own answer, so never add an \"Other\" option. If the questions stop mattering, or you decide yourself, withdraw them by calling ask_user with withdraw set to true, and say what you decided. Read the answers carefully and follow what they actually say; if the user skipped a question, decide yourself and say what you assumed.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"questions":{"description":"The questions, asked together.","items":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"header":{"description":"A short tag shown above the question, at most 12 characters, such as \"Database\".","type":"string"},"multi_select":{"description":"Let the user choose more than one option. Defaults to false.","type":"boolean"},"options":{"description":"The choices.","items":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"description":{"description":"What choosing this means, in one sentence.","type":"string"},"label":{"description":"The choice, in one to five words.","type":"string"}},"required":["description","label"],"type":"object"},"maxItems":4,"minItems":2,"type":"array"},"question":{"description":"The whole question, ending with a question mark.","type":"string"}},"required":["header","options","question"],"type":"object"},"maxItems":4,"minItems":1,"type":"array"},"withdraw":{"description":"Set to true, with no other field, to withdraw your open questions instead of asking: the user no longer sees them.","type":"boolean"}},"type":"object"},"name":"ask_user"},{"description":"Run a bash command on the user's computer and get its exit code and combined stdout and stderr. The working directory carries over between calls (a cd persists); variables, aliases and functions do not. Commands run as the user with no sandbox and no approval step. By default a command runs in the foreground with empty stdin and no terminal, and the call waits for it to finish, so don't run editors, pagers or anything that prompts that way: pass flags such as --yes or --no-pager, or set tty. A command is stopped when it runs longer than timeout_ms, 120000 by default; give a larger value for slow builds or tests, or 0 for no time limit. A foreground command still running after 2 minutes is handed back to you as a numbered process with its output so far; it keeps running, and its time limit still applies. Set tty to run the command in a terminal, for REPLs, prompts, full-screen programs or other agents' terminal interfaces; in the foreground, a terminal command also returns once its screen has been still for 10 seconds, since it may be waiting for input. Set run_in_background, with or without tty, to start a server, a watcher or any long job and get its process number within a few seconds; it then has no time limit unless you give timeout_ms. For REPLs and other programs you will keep talking to, use run_in_background, or timeout_ms 0 in the foreground. Never ask the user to paste a password or other secret into the chat, where it would reach you and the session log; when a command needs one, call ask_secret, which has the user enter it in a box and types it into the process without showing it to you; when a terminal command needs something else only a person can do, such as a browser login or a program you can't drive, call process with ask_user_to_attach so they type into it directly. Check one variable by name and list processes by number and name; don't print the whole environment or processes' arguments (env, ps aux), where any secret could reach you and the session log. When a background process ends while no turn is running, you are told in a new turn, so end your turn rather than wait for a long job. A result that gives a process number means the process tool can wait for it, read or search its output, show its screen, type into it or stop it. Programs left running with & or nohup are stopped when the command that started them exits; run them with run_in_background instead. Background processes are stopped when Wisp exits; for a server or other service that must stay up after that, also set keep_running. The full output of every call is saved to a file whose path is in the result; long output is shown only as a preview, so read the parts you need with the process tool, or from that file with sed -n, grep or tail.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"command":{"description":"The bash command or script to run.","type":"string"},"description":{"description":"Five to ten words on what the command does, shown to the user.","type":"string"},"keep_running":{"description":"With run_in_background: keep the process running after Wisp exits, for a server or service that must stay up. Its output then goes to a log file named in the result.","type":"boolean"},"run_in_background":{"description":"Start the command and return within a few seconds with its process number and first output, instead of waiting for it to finish. Without tty its stdin stays open for the process tool. It is stopped when Wisp exits unless keep_running is set.","type":"boolean"},"timeout_ms":{"description":"The command's time limit in milliseconds: it is stopped once it has run this long. 0 means no limit. Defaults to 120000 in the foreground and to no limit with run_in_background. A foreground call returns after 2 minutes at most; a longer limit keeps applying to the process it hands back.","type":"integer"},"tty":{"description":"Run the command in a terminal, so it can prompt, draw a full screen and take keys through the process tool. Combine with run_in_background to start it without waiting.","type":"boolean"}},"required":["command"],"type":"object"},"name":"bash"},{"description":"List the accounts the user has set up in Wisp, as /account, /model and /usage show them: each account's name and kind, whether it can be used and is signed in, which one you run on and where new chats start, the models it offers and, for a subscription, the usage limits Wisp last read. Keys and tokens are never shown.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{},"type":"object"},"name":"list_accounts"},{"description":"List the agents you can reach: your parent, your siblings and every agent below you, each with its path, preset, model and effort, state, spend, checklist progress, working directory and the first line of its last report. Use it to find an agent's path or check how the work is spread, for example after older turns were summarised. Don't call it to wait for a child: its report arrives by itself.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{},"type":"object"},"name":"list_agents"},{"description":"List Wisp's settings as /config shows them for this chat: each setting's value and where it comes from (default, config, env, flag, session and so on). With filter, also what each matching setting does and the values it takes. Secrets are never shown. Changing a setting is the user's to do: point them to /config.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"filter":{"description":"Text to look for in a setting's name, label or help, such as theme or compact; leave it out to list every setting.","type":"string"}},"type":"object"},"name":"list_settings"},{"description":"Work with a process that a bash result gave a number: one started with run_in_background, handed back while waiting in a terminal, moved to the background by the user, or finished with more to read than its result showed. Give id to act on one; omit it to list this session's processes. Without input, keys, signal or wait_ms it answers at once with the output that is new since you last looked. timeout_ms gives the process a new time limit counted from now, or 0 for none. read tail shows the last lines, read lines a range from a line number, read search the lines matching a regular expression anywhere in the output, with their line numbers, and read screen a terminal's current screen. Waiting with wait_ms (up to 600000) costs one call however long it lasts, so prefer one long wait with until or quiet_ms over repeated short calls, or end your turn: you are told when a process ends. Line numbers count from 1 over the whole output and match the output file, whose path the results give, so they also work with sed -n on it. ask_user_to_attach asks the user to type into a terminal process themselves, for a login or a program you can't drive, and waits until they are done; for a password or any other secret call ask_secret instead, and never ask for one in the chat.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"ask_user_to_attach":{"description":"Ask the user to type into this terminal process themselves, for anything only a person can do other than entering a secret, which ask_secret does, with a short reason shown to them. The call waits until they have typed and detached, decline, or the process ends.","type":"string"},"context":{"description":"With read search: how many lines to show before and after each match, 0 to 10. Defaults to 0.","type":"integer"},"description":{"description":"Five to ten words on what this call is for, shown to the user, such as Check the dev server's output or Ask the reviewer for its findings. Give one on every call, and describe the purpose, not the text you type.","type":"string"},"from":{"description":"With read lines: the first line to show. With read search: the first line to search, to continue after an earlier search's last match. Defaults to 1.","type":"integer"},"id":{"description":"The process number from a bash result.","type":"integer"},"input":{"description":"Text to write to the process's stdin, exactly as given; no newline is added. A terminal in line mode keeps at most 4095 bytes per line, so a longer line is refused there: write long text to a file, or use a process without tty.","type":"string"},"keys":{"description":"Keys to press after input, by name: Enter, Tab, Space, Esc, Backspace, Delete, Up, Down, Left, Right, Home, End, PageUp, PageDown, F1 to F12, C-a to C-z (C-c interrupts and C-d ends input in a terminal) and EOF, which closes stdin.","items":{"type":"string"},"type":"array"},"lines":{"description":"With read tail or lines: how many lines to show. With read search: the most matching lines to show, at most 500. Defaults to 50 for tail and search and 100 for lines.","type":"integer"},"pattern":{"description":"With read search: a regular expression matched against each line; (?i) at the start ignores case.","type":"string"},"quiet_ms":{"description":"Stop waiting once the output (or the terminal screen) has not changed for this long. Defaults to 1000 after input or keys and 500 after signal interrupt.","type":"integer"},"read":{"description":"What to show: new (output since you last looked; the default), tail (the last lines), lines (from a line number), search (lines matching pattern), or screen (a terminal's current screen; the default for a terminal showing a full-screen program).","enum":["new","tail","lines","search","screen"],"type":"string"},"signal":{"description":"interrupt is Ctrl-C: in a terminal it reaches the program in the foreground, otherwise every process the command started gets SIGINT. kill stops the process and everything it started.","enum":["interrupt","kill"],"type":"string"},"size":{"description":"Resize a terminal, as COLUMNSxROWS such as 120x40.","type":"string"},"timeout_ms":{"description":"A new time limit for the process in milliseconds, counted from now; 0 removes its limit.","type":"integer"},"until":{"description":"A regular expression: stop waiting as soon as new output, or a line of the terminal screen, matches it.","type":"string"},"wait_ms":{"description":"The longest to wait, in milliseconds, at most 600000, for the process to exit, until to match or quiet_ms to pass. Defaults to 30000 after input or keys, 2000 after signal interrupt, otherwise 0.","type":"integer"}},"type":"object"},"name":"process"},{"description":"Save or forget a note when the user asks. Save a durable fact, not instructions, task state, private details or facts already in the repository. The same name in the same scope replaces the note.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"action":{"enum":["save","forget"],"type":"string"},"body":{"description":"Optional Markdown detail for save only; the whole note fits in 4 KiB.","type":"string"},"fact":{"description":"For save, one line up to 240 UTF-8 bytes. Omit for forget.","type":"string"},"name":{"description":"A lowercase slug for the note.","maxLength":48,"minLength":1,"pattern":"^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$","type":"string"},"scope":{"enum":["you","project"],"type":"string"}},"required":["action","scope","name"],"type":"object"},"name":"remember"},{"description":"Rename an agent below you when the user asks you to: one of your children by its name, or a deeper agent by its path. It keeps running and its own agents move with it. Its old name still reaches it and is never given to another agent.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"name":{"description":"A child's name, or the path of an agent further below you.","type":"string"},"new_name":{"description":"Its new name: a lowercase letter, then up to 31 of a-z, 0-9, _ and -.","type":"string"}},"required":["name","new_name"],"type":"object"},"name":"rename_agent"},{"description":"Look for tools, skills and documents you need. Use the words their descriptions would use, not synonyms. Tool hits tell you how to run them; read skills and documents with use.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"kind":{"description":"Restrict results to this kind; omit to search all kinds.","enum":["tool","skill","document"],"type":"string"},"limit":{"description":"Maximum results; five by default.","minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"query":{"description":"Words describing what you need.","type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"type":"object"},"name":"search"},{"description":"Send a message to another agent: your parent (`..`), one of your children (its name), a sibling (`../name`) or any agent below you (its path, as list_agents shows it). You can't message your parent's parent or a sibling's children: ask your parent to pass it on. The recipient sees only this text, never your conversation, so make it complete. A running recipient reads it at its next step; an idle one is woken to read it, unless wake is false, when it waits until the recipient next runs. A message with wake on to your own child is a new task, and the child reports back when it is done. To ask your parent or a sibling something, send the question and end your reply: the answer arrives as a message, so don't poll for it. Siblings can exchange only a limited number of messages before their parent writes to them again. Hand in your own result with report, not with a message.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"text":{"description":"The message, complete in itself.","type":"string"},"to":{"description":"The recipient: `..` for your parent, a child's name, `../name` for a sibling, or the path of an agent below you.","type":"string"},"wake":{"description":"false lets an idle recipient read it when it next runs instead of waking it. true when left out.","type":"boolean"}},"required":["to","text"],"type":"object"},"name":"send_message"},{"description":"Show the user code in the chat, with a button that copies it. Use it for code the user will copy and use: a command to run, a file to save, a snippet to paste. It only displays the code: it runs nothing and changes no files. The user sees it with syntax colours, so don't repeat it in your reply. For a line or two inside an explanation, a Markdown code block is enough.\n\nGive `language` for syntax colours, such as rust or bash. Limits: 10,000 lines and 512 KiB.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"code":{"description":"The code, exactly as the user should copy it.","type":"string"},"language":{"description":"The language for syntax colours, such as rust or bash.","type":"string"},"title":{"description":"A short heading, such as what the code does.","type":"string"}},"required":["code"],"type":"object"},"name":"show_code"},{"description":"Show the user a diff in the chat. Use it when you want the user to see a change: one you propose, one you made, or a comparison they asked for. It only displays the diff: it changes no files and runs nothing, so it never applies a change. The user sees the diff with changed words and syntax highlighted, so don't repeat it in your reply.\n\nGive each file as `before` and `after` (the old and new text; Wisp computes the diff and shows 3 unchanged lines around each change) or as `diff` (a unified diff you wrote; wrong hunk line counts are recounted, and hunk headers without line numbers are accepted). Give `path` so the code gets syntax colours, or `language` when there is no path. Limits: 20 files, 10,000 lines and 512 KiB for each side of a file, 1 MiB in all.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"files":{"description":"The files to show, in order, one entry per file.","items":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"after":{"description":"The new text. Use \"\" for a deleted file.","type":"string"},"before":{"description":"The old text. Use \"\" for a new file.","type":"string"},"diff":{"description":"A unified diff of this file, instead of before and after.","type":"string"},"language":{"description":"The language for syntax colours when path doesn't show it, such as rust.","type":"string"},"path":{"description":"The file's path, shown to the user.","type":"string"}},"type":"object"},"maxItems":20,"minItems":1,"type":"array"},"title":{"description":"A short heading for the diff, such as what the change does.","type":"string"}},"required":["files"],"type":"object"},"name":"show_diff"},{"description":"Start a named child agent on a task. It works in the background with its own fresh context and reports back when it is done; the call returns at once. Use it for work that can go on by itself while you do something else: an independent investigation, a review of a change it didn't write, or a self-contained change. Do small or tightly linked steps yourself, and don't let two agents edit the same files. The child sees only its task, the project's instructions and what it finds itself, never your conversation, so write the task as a complete brief: the goal, the facts and files that matter, what to leave alone and what to hand back. It has your endpoint, model, effort and tools by default, except that it can't ask the user (it asks you instead); endpoint, model and effort may change that, tools_off and spawn only narrow it, and preset names a set of those choices, which may include instructions for the child's role. Its report arrives later as a message from it: don't poll it, wait for it or redo its work, but carry on with other work or end your reply. To run several children at once, start them in the same reply.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"cwd":{"description":"The child's working directory: absolute, under ~/ or relative to yours. Yours when left out.","type":"string"},"effort":{"description":"The child's effort. Leave it out to use the preset's, or else yours.","enum":["low","medium","high","xhigh","max"],"type":"string"},"endpoint":{"description":"One of the session's endpoints for the child, by name. Set it only when the user asks for a particular endpoint or provider. Leave it out to use the preset's, or else yours.","type":"string"},"keep_warm":{"description":"true if you will message it again after its report; while it idles, Wisp refreshes its prompt cache a limited number of times. End it when you won't.","type":"boolean"},"model":{"description":"Another model for the child, one its endpoint serves. Leave it out to use the preset's, or else yours.","type":"string"},"name":{"description":"The child's name, unique among your children for the whole session: a lowercase letter, then up to 31 of a-z, 0-9, _ and -. Its path is yours plus this name.","type":"string"},"preset":{"description":"A preset listed in your <environment>; this call's own fields override its choices.","type":"string"},"spawn":{"description":"false stops the child from starting agents of its own.","type":"boolean"},"task":{"description":"The brief: everything the child needs to do the task without your conversation, and what it should hand back.","type":"string"},"todos":{"description":"Optional steps of the task, in order; they become the child's starting checklist.","items":{"type":"string"},"type":"array"},"tools_off":{"description":"Names of your tools the child shouldn't have.","items":{"type":"string"},"type":"array"}},"required":["name","task"],"type":"object"},"name":"spawn_agent"},{"description":"Stop an agent below you: one of your children by its name, or a deeper agent by its path. Its running turn ends at once, and so does the turn of every agent below it; they stay idle with their context, and a message wakes them again. Each agent stopped below it tells its own parent. Set end to true once you no longer need it: it and every agent below it are retired, take no more messages, stop counting toward the limit on live agents and have their background processes stopped. Don't stop a child to wait for it, and don't end one whose report you still need.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"end":{"description":"true also retires it and every agent below it for good. false when left out.","type":"boolean"},"name":{"description":"A child's name, or the path of an agent further below you.","type":"string"}},"required":["name"],"type":"object"},"name":"stop_agent"},{"description":"Open a skill or document found by search, or run a found tool with its input.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"input":{"description":"Arguments for a tool; omit when reading a skill or document.","type":"object"},"name":{"description":"The full name returned by search.","type":"string"}},"required":["name"],"type":"object"},"name":"use"},{"description":"Attach a file from the user's computer to this tool's result so you can inspect it in your next step: a screenshot you took, a chart or page a script drew, a document, a log or a source file. Text comes back as text: up to 2,000 lines and 50 KiB, or else the start of the file and the bash command that reads the next part. Any other file is attached whole, as it is, with its media type, and the provider decides whether it can read it. If it can't, or the file is too large, the result says so: convert or cut it with bash (a smaller image, the pages you need, extracted text) and view that instead. Keep images you attach at most 2,000 px on their long edge; resize a copy with bash first. You may pass at most one of `pages`, `seconds` or `sheet` to ask a supporting model to read only that part of a document, audio/video file or spreadsheet. Models that don't support slices refuse locally without sending the file; when a sliced read is accepted, the whole file is still uploaded, stored and resent in the conversation until compaction. To keep the rest of a private or oversized file out of the request, cut out the part you need with bash and view that copy. Attached files stay in the conversation, so view only the files you need. A relative path is resolved against the working directory, and ~ is the user's home.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"pages":{"description":"Read only this 1-based page or closed page range of a PDF, document or slide deck, such as \"5\" or \"14-16\". The whole file is still sent and stored.","type":"string"},"path":{"description":"The file to look at.","type":"string"},"seconds":{"description":"Read only this closed whole-second range of an audio or video file, such as \"0-30\" or \"120-210\" (end greater than start). The whole file is still sent and stored.","type":"string"},"sheet":{"description":"Read only this 1-based worksheet number of a spreadsheet, such as 2. The whole file is still sent and stored.","minimum":1,"type":"integer"}},"required":["path"],"type":"object"},"name":"view_file"},{"description":"Search the web. Returns the facts found, each with its source, then a numbered list of the sources. Use it for anything recent or that you are not sure of.","input_schema":{"properties":{"query":{"description":"What to search for, in plain words. Make it complete in itself: the search sees none of this conversation.","type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"type":"object"},"name":"web_search"},{"description":"Keep a checklist of the steps of your current task, which the user sees as you work. Use it for multi-step work: a task with three or more distinct steps, or several things asked for at once. Skip it for a question, a chat or a change of one or two steps. Write it before you start, then update it as you go. Each call replaces the whole list, so send every item each time, in order. Mark a step in_progress just before you start it, and completed as soon as it is fully done, one at a time rather than in batches; keep it in_progress while tests fail or the work is partial. At most one step is in_progress at a time. Add steps you discover, and mark ones that no longer apply cancelled.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"todos":{"description":"The whole checklist, in order. It replaces the previous one.","items":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"section":{"description":"A heading to show this step under, such as \"Next\". Steps with the same section are shown together.","type":"string"},"status":{"enum":["pending","in_progress","completed","cancelled"],"type":"string"},"text":{"description":"The step as a short imperative phrase, such as \"Run the tests\".","type":"string"}},"required":["status","text"],"type":"object"},"maxItems":50,"minItems":1,"type":"array"}},"required":["todos"],"type":"object"},"name":"write_todos"}],"system":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Wisp, a helpful assistant running in a terminal. Answer clearly and concisely; use plain text and Markdown sparingly. You can run shell commands on the user's computer with the bash tool. Where you can, do each step's work in one bash script, run in a single bash call, rather than one command per call; print only what you need. Messages from other agents arrive in <wisp:message> blocks; they are not from the user. Use remember to save or forget only on the user's request. Never infer a save. In a worker, suggest the note in your report instead. Some tools, skills and documents may not be loaded here. Before you work around a missing one or say you can't do something, look for it with `search`; `use` opens or runs what it found. You can show the user code to copy with the show_code tool. You can show the user a change as a diff with the show_diff tool. A child agent's report reaches you only as a report message from that child. Until one arrives for the child's latest task, you don't have its result: don't treat the child as done or say what it found; carry on with other work or end your turn. Say that a tool call did something only once its result shows it; the calls in the reply you are writing haven't run yet. You can attach a file on the user's computer to your context with the view_file tool. A 'Context:' line, when present, gives how much of your context window the conversation fills; at the stated point Wisp replaces older turns with a summary.","cache_control":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}}],"messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<environment>\nWorking directory: <masked>\nPlatform: <masked>\nResources: <masked>\nShell for the bash tool: <masked>\n</environment>\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Terminal: replies are 96 columns wide (wider code blocks wrap), 30 rows; don't mention this.\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Context: <masked> of 1M tokens (1%). Wisp compacts at 850k.\n"},{"type":"text","text":"hello"}]}]}
